\section{The two conventions for \code{region}}{
A parcellation that names its structures anatomically gives both
hemispheres the same name, so \code{region} aggregates them and \code{hemi}
disambiguates: grouping by \code{region} gives one row per structure.

A parcellation that numbers its parcels does not. \code{region_0001} and
\code{region_0002} are two different parcels, not one structure seen twice,
and no hemisphere-free name exists to recover -- the source never gave
one. There \code{region} is 1:1 with \code{label} and grouping by it aggregates
nothing.

Both are correct for their source. What was missing was a way to ask
which you have, short of testing whether any name is shared across
hemispheres. \code{\link[=atlas_region_semantics]{atlas_region_semantics()}} is that question.
